WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.812
This note says 100 trillion dollars.

00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:05.856
Zimbabwe issued it in January 2009, when

00:00:05.866 --> 00:00:08.254
prices could double every day.

00:00:08.354 --> 00:00:11.817
Yearly inflation was
estimated at around 230

00:00:11.827 --> 00:00:13.257
million percent.

00:00:13.357 --> 00:00:14.542
And it got worse:

00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:16.566
in late 2008, a loaf of bread

00:00:16.576 --> 00:00:19.233
cost millions of Zimbabwe dollars.

00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:21.048
Weeks later, billions.

00:00:21.148 --> 00:00:24.011
By April 2009, Zimbabwe had dropped its

00:00:24.021 --> 00:00:26.883
own currency for foreign money, such as

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.031
the US dollar.

00:00:28.131 --> 00:00:31.631
In 2015, the government let people trade

00:00:31.641 --> 00:00:33.014
in the old notes.

00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:35.939
So what was Z$100 trillion worth?

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:37.022
40 US cents.

00:00:37.032 --> 00:00:40.992
One woman's Z$90 trillion
wouldn't even cover

00:00:41.002 --> 00:00:42.696
her bus fare home.
